auth_expired.description:
Authentication for Google Find My Device is invalid or has expired for this entry.

**Entry:** {entry_title}
**Account:** {email}

Select **Reconfigure** on the integration card to sign in again. If you recently changed your Google password or revoked tokens, you must re-authenticate here to restore functionality.

**Tip:** Google may revoke tokens when requests come from a different IP address or region than where the token was created (e.g. token generated on a laptop but used from a server or VPS in another country). Generate your `secrets.json` on the same network where Home Assistant runs, or use the same public IP address.

duplicate_account_entries.description:
This Google account (**{email}**) is already configured by another entry:

{entries}

Disable or remove the duplicate entry so that only one entry per account remains active, then reload the integration (or restart Home Assistant).

tracker_attributes_frozen.description:
The device trackers of **{entry_title}** no longer update the attributes `location_age` and `location_status` between observations: they keep the value of the last observation and change only with the next one (for example a new fix, the change to stale, or an availability change). The main tracker keeps the `coarse_*` attributes the same way, and the Last Location tracker no longer has them. A tracker without a new fix therefore no longer looks fresher than another one, for example to a person.

For the live values, use these entities:
{replacement_sensors}

These attributes will be removed, at the latest with the first integration release from Home Assistant {breaks_version} on.

Automations and scripts that use these trackers (please check them for `location_status`, `location_age` and `coarse_*`):
{automations}
